// Pocket — the timer block. FF04 DIV, FF05 TIMA, FF06 TMA, FF07 TAC.
//
// A transliteration of the reference emulator/core/timer.ts. Behaviour is
// identical; only the storage moved, from class fields into the arrays
// gb_state.logic declares:
//
//   this.divCounter            -> GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV]        16-bit, free-running
//   this.tima                  -> GB_IO[0x05]             canonical
//   this.tma                   -> GB_IO[0x06]             canonical
//   this.tac                   -> GB_IO[0x07]             canonical, low 3 bits
//   this.timerBitWasHigh       -> GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT]      0 or 1, and
//                                 DERIVED from DIV rather than remembered;
//                                 see the note above gbTimer_syncLive
//   this.overflowPendingCycles -> GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMA_PENDING]   T-cycles left
//   this.reloadJustHappened    -> GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_RELOADED] 0 or 1
//
// GB_IO[0x04] is unused: DIV is always derived from the top byte of
// GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV], never stored. gbCpu_reset seeds that counter with 0xABCC on
// DMG or 0x2680 on CGB, which is why the post-boot DIV reads as it does.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THIS IS AN EDGE DETECTOR AND NOT A DIVIDE-BY-N
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// TIMA is not clocked by a periodic counter. It is clocked by the FALLING EDGE
// of (DIV_bit AND TAC.enable), where DIV_bit is [9, 3, 5, 7][TAC & 3] of the
// internal 16-bit counter:
//
//   TAC&3 = 00   bit 9   every 1024 T-cycles     4096 Hz
//   TAC&3 = 01   bit 3   every   16 T-cycles   262144 Hz
//   TAC&3 = 10   bit 5   every   64 T-cycles    65536 Hz
//   TAC&3 = 11   bit 7   every  256 T-cycles    16384 Hz
//
// Written as a divide-by-N it looks the same until something moves the input
// out from under it. Because it is an edge detector, three things fall out for
// free, and all three are load-bearing:
//
//   * Writing DIV while the selected bit is high drops that bit to zero, which
//     is a falling edge, which increments TIMA. That is the DMG quirk Pokemon's
//     RNG rests on — a game that writes FF04 in a tight loop gets a TIMA that
//     advances with it.
//   * Writing TAC to select a different bit, or to clear the enable, can
//     likewise present a falling edge and tick TIMA once.
//   * The phase of the increment is the phase of the DIV counter, not of the
//     last write, so resetting DIV mid-period lengthens or shortens the period
//     exactly as hardware does.
//
// So every path that can change DIV or TAC re-evaluates the bit and applies the
// edge: gbTimer_step, gbTimer_writeDiv and gbTimer_writeTac.
//
// gbTimer_step does NOT walk every T-cycle to find those edges. The bit is high
// for the top half of its period and low for the bottom half, so its falling
// edges are the multiples of that period the counter crosses, and the count of
// them over a span is two shifts and a subtract. The cycle-by-cycle loop is
// still there and is still what runs across a TIMA overflow, where the reload
// window has to open on the exact cycle.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE OVERFLOW WINDOW
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// TIMA does not reload the instant it wraps FF -> 00. For four T-cycles it
// reads 00, no interrupt has fired, and TMA has not been copied in. At the end
// of that window TIMA takes TMA, the timer IRQ is raised, and a one-M-cycle
// "just reloaded" window opens. Inside those two windows writes behave oddly,
// and that is what the Mooneye tima_write_reloading / tma_write_reloading tests
// measure:
//
//   write TIMA during the 4-cycle window   cancels the reload and the IRQ,
//                                          and the written value sticks
//   write TIMA on the reload M-cycle       dropped; TIMA keeps the TMA value
//   write TMA  on the reload M-cycle       adopted; TIMA takes the new TMA
//
// GB_C_TIMER_RELOADED is cleared at the top of gbTimer_step, and by the quiet
// span in gbMachine_tickParts on the instructions where it stands in for a run
// of gbTimer_step calls. Either way it is visible to exactly the writes that
// happen between one tick and the next. Pocket does not do per-M-cycle memory access timing (CONTRACT §3.3),
// so a write landing inside a long instruction is attributed to the instruction
// boundary; that is the documented accuracy loss and it does not change any of
// the windows above.
//
// TIMA reads need no special case anywhere: during the 4-cycle window the byte
// in GB_IO[0x05] genuinely is 0x00, because that is what it wrapped to.

// The DIV bit each TAC mode watches, as a pre-shifted mask: bits 9, 3, 5, 7.
// A typed array marshals to an opaque slot and is skipped by the state sync, so
// this costs nothing per host call, and the mask saves a shift per T-cycle.
let gbTimer_DIVMASK = Uint16Array.from([0x0200, 0x0008, 0x0020, 0x0080])

// log2 of the FULL period of each of those bits — one high half plus one low
// half, so twice the mask: 1024, 16, 64, 256 T-cycles. Used by the counted
// path in gbTimer_step to turn "how many falling edges are in this span" into
// two shifts and a subtract. A shift rather than a divide because every period
// is a power of two and this is the only arithmetic on the path.
let gbTimer_DIVSHIFT = Uint8Array.from([10, 4, 6, 8])

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE LIVE FLAG
//
// 1 when gbTimer_step has anything at all to do beyond counting DIV up: TAC's
// enable bit is set, or a stale high bit is still waiting to fall, or a reload
// is draining. It is a pure function of
//
//     (GB_IO[0x07] & 0x04), GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT], GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMA_PENDING]
//
// and holds no information of its own, so it is not in a save state and is
// recomputed on load.
//
// It exists because it is READ once per instruction by gb_machine's quiet-span
// test and once per call here, and a plain scalar reads in ~2ns on the shipping
// engine against ~22ns for an Int32Array element — three reads become one, and
// the arithmetic that derived them disappears.
//
// gb_timer is the only module that writes it. Every path that can move any of
// the three inputs ends in gbTimer_syncLive(): writeDiv, writeTac and writeTima
// here, plus the slow half of gbTimer_step. gbTimer_writeTma is deliberately
// not in that list — it can move TIMA, but none of the three inputs.
// gbState_reset and gbMachine_loadState write the arrays behind this file's
// back and call gbTimer_syncLive() for that reason.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
let gbTimer_live = 0

// T-cycles from where DIV stands now to the next moment gbTimer_step could do
// anything: the next falling edge of the selected bit, or the end of a reload
// window. 0x40000000 when the timer is off, which is the same "parked far
// enough out that it never fires" trick gbPpu_lcdOff uses on GB_PPU_DOTS.
//
// This is what gbMachine_tickParts's quiet span actually tests. A running
// timer at 4096Hz has a falling edge once every 1024 T-cycles — about once in
// a hundred instructions — so testing "is an edge due" rather than "is the
// timer on" keeps the span open for the other ninety-nine, and DIV goes up by
// a plain add exactly as it does with the timer switched off.
//
// It is only ever allowed to be too SMALL. A value below the truth costs a
// call to gbTimer_step, which is always correct; a value above it would skip
// an edge. Every branch below that cannot prove a distance sets it to 0.
let gbTimer_edgeIn = 0x40000000

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY GB_C_TIMER_BIT IS NOW DERIVED AND NOT REMEMBERED
//
// The reference keeps timerBitWasHigh as a memory: the previous value of the
// bit, compared against the new one every T-cycle. That only works if EVERY
// T-cycle passes through this file, and the quiet span in gb_machine
// deliberately does not — it advances DIV by a whole instruction without
// calling in here at all, so a rising edge inside the span would leave the
// remembered value behind and the next falling edge would be missed.
//
// While the enable is set, though, the bit is not independent information: it
// is (DIV & mask) and nothing else. So the slot is written from DIV at every
// point that looks at it, and the two places that genuinely need the PREVIOUS
// value — a DIV write and a TAC write, where the register moves out from under
// the detector — take it before the write instead of out of storage. The slot
// stays in GB_CTR because it is in the save state and because it still carries
// one thing DIV cannot: a bit left high after the enable was cleared, which is
// the only case the derivation below leaves alone.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
function gbTimer_syncLive() {
  let tac = GB_IO[0x07]
  let pending = GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMA_PENDING]
  let div = GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV]

  let mask = 0
  if ((tac & 0x04) !== 0) { mask = gbTimer_DIVMASK[tac & 0x03] }

  // With the enable set the stored bit is redundant with DIV, so refresh it.
  // With the enable clear it is the one thing DIV does not know, so leave it.
  if (mask !== 0) {
    if ((div & mask) !== 0) {
      GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT] = 1
    } else {
      GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT] = 0
    }
  }

  if (mask === 0 && GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT] === 0 && pending === 0) {
    gbTimer_live = 0
    gbTimer_edgeIn = 0x40000000
    return
  }

  gbTimer_live = 1

  // A reload is draining, or the enable is clear with a stale high bit still to
  // fall. Neither has a distance that can be computed here, so hand the next
  // step to the loop.
  if (pending !== 0 || mask === 0) {
    gbTimer_edgeIn = 0
    return
  }

  // The bit falls when the counter reaches the next multiple of its full
  // period, and the period is a power of two, so the distance is one mask.
  let period = mask << 1
  gbTimer_edgeIn = period - (div & (period - 1))
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Register access
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// DIV is the upper byte of the internal counter.
function gbTimer_readDiv() {
  return (GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV] >> 8) & 0xff
}

function gbTimer_readTima() {
  return GB_IO[0x05]
}

function gbTimer_readTma() {
  return GB_IO[0x06]
}

// Only three bits exist; the rest read back high.
function gbTimer_readTac() {
  return GB_IO[0x07] | 0xf8
}

// Any write to FF04, whatever the value, clears the whole 16-bit counter. If
// the selected bit was high, that is a falling edge and TIMA ticks.
// The "before" bit is taken from the registers as they stand rather than from
// GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT], because the quiet span may have moved DIV under it
// since this file last looked; see the note above gbTimer_syncLive. With the
// registers unchanged the two are the same value, so this is the same edge the
// reference detects.
function gbTimer_writeDiv() {
  let before = gbTimer_currentBit()
  GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV] = 0
  let after = gbTimer_currentBit()
  if (before !== 0 && after === 0) { gbTimer_incTima() }
  // Stored explicitly, because gbTimer_syncLive leaves the slot alone when the
  // enable is clear and this write may be what cleared it.
  GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT] = after
  gbTimer_syncLive()
}

// Changing the selected bit, or clearing the enable while the bit is high,
// presents a falling edge to the same detector. `before` is read with the OLD
// TAC still in place, which is the whole point of the quirk.
function gbTimer_writeTac(v) {
  let before = gbTimer_currentBit()
  GB_IO[0x07] = v & 0x07
  let after = gbTimer_currentBit()
  if (before !== 0 && after === 0) { gbTimer_incTima() }
  // Stored explicitly, because gbTimer_syncLive leaves the slot alone when the
  // enable is clear and this write may be what cleared it.
  GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT] = after
  gbTimer_syncLive()
}

// TIMA writes are special-cased inside the two overflow windows; see the header.
function gbTimer_writeTima(v) {
  if (GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMA_PENDING] > 0) {
    // Inside the 4-cycle window: the pending reload and its IRQ are cancelled
    // outright and the written value is what TIMA keeps.
    GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMA_PENDING] = 0
    GB_IO[0x05] = v & 0xff
    gbTimer_syncLive()
    return
  }
  if (GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_RELOADED] !== 0) {
    // On the M-cycle after the reload the write is swallowed; TIMA stays at TMA.
    return
  }
  GB_IO[0x05] = v & 0xff
}

// TMA written on the reload M-cycle is picked up by TIMA as well as by TMA.
function gbTimer_writeTma(v) {
  GB_IO[0x06] = v & 0xff
  if (GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_RELOADED] !== 0) {
    GB_IO[0x05] = v & 0xff
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The edge detector
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// (DIV_bit AND TAC.enable), as 0 or 1.
function gbTimer_currentBit() {
  if ((GB_IO[0x07] & 0x04) === 0) { return 0 }
  if ((GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV] & gbTimer_DIVMASK[GB_IO[0x07] & 0x03]) !== 0) { return 1 }
  return 0
}

// One TIMA increment. Wrapping to zero opens the 4-cycle overflow window; the
// reload and the IRQ happen at the end of it, in gbTimer_step.
function gbTimer_incTima() {
  let t = (GB_IO[0x05] + 1) & 0xff
  GB_IO[0x05] = t
  if (t === 0) {
    GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMA_PENDING] = 4
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The tick
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Advance by `cycles` T-cycles. gbMachine_tickParts calls this first of the
// four peripherals and in 4-cycle chunks, so the loop below normally runs four
// times — cheap enough to keep per-cycle, and per-cycle is the only way to see
// every falling edge of DIV bit 3 (one edge per 16 T-cycles) and to drain the
// overflow window on the right cycle.
//
// TAC cannot change during a step — nothing inside this function writes it, and
// the CPU writes it between steps — so the enable test and the bit selection
// are hoisted out of the loop. Inside the loop the only input is DIV.
function gbTimer_step(cycles) {
  // The reload window lasts until the next tick and no longer. Clearing it here
  // rather than at the end of the previous step is what makes it visible to the
  // writes that happen in between.
  GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_RELOADED] = 0

  // Timer off, no stale high bit to fall, nothing draining: the loop would do
  // nothing but count DIV up, so count it up in one go. Most of a frame in a
  // game that never enables the timer takes this path. gbTimer_live is exactly
  // the disjunction the three tests used to spell out, cached in a scalar.
  if (gbTimer_live === 0) {
    GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV] = (GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV] + cycles) & 0xffff
    return
  }

  let tac = GB_IO[0x07]
  let mask = 0
  if ((tac & 0x04) !== 0) { mask = gbTimer_DIVMASK[tac & 0x03] }

  let div = GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV]
  let pending = GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMA_PENDING]

  // Derived from DIV while the enable is set, because the quiet span in
  // gb_machine advances DIV without coming through here and the stored slot is
  // therefore only a mirror; see the note above gbTimer_syncLive. With the
  // enable clear the slot is the only record of a bit left high, so it stands.
  let was = GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT]
  if (mask !== 0) {
    was = 0
    if ((div & mask) !== 0) { was = 1 }
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // THE COUNTED PATH
  //
  // With the enable on and nothing draining, the loop below is a very
  // expensive way to count something arithmetic. The selected bit is high for
  // the top half of a
  // period and low for the bottom half, so it FALLS exactly when the counter
  // crosses a multiple of that period, and the number of crossings in
  // (div, div+cycles] is the difference of the two counters shifted down by
  // log2(period). TIMA advances by that many, and the bit ends up wherever the
  // new counter puts it.
  //
  // The wrap at 0x10000 does not disturb the count: every period (16, 64, 256,
  // 1024) divides 65536, so a multiple of the period is crossed at the wrap
  // itself and the unwrapped difference is the wrapped one.
  //
  // Only an overflow needs the cycle-by-cycle version, because the four-cycle
  // reload window has to open on the right cycle — and that is one call in
  // every 256 edges. gbTimer_live is not re-derived here: mask is non-zero, so
  // TAC's enable bit is set, so live is 1 either way.
  //
  // This is what lets gbMachine_tickParts take its quiet span on a machine
  // whose timer is RUNNING. Without it, every game that enables the timer —
  // which is most of them once a link cable or a music driver is involved —
  // falls back to 4-cycle chunking for the whole frame.
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  if (pending === 0 && mask !== 0) {
    let shift = gbTimer_DIVSHIFT[tac & 0x03]
    let next = div + cycles
    let tima = GB_IO[0x05] + ((next >>> shift) - (div >>> shift))
    if (tima < 256) {
      GB_IO[0x05] = tima
      next = next & 0xffff
      GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV] = next
      if ((next & mask) !== 0) {
        GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT] = 1
      } else {
        GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT] = 0
      }
      // Re-arm the distance to the next edge from where the counter now
      // stands. gbTimer_live cannot have changed — mask is non-zero, so the
      // enable bit is set — so only this half of gbTimer_syncLive is needed.
      let period = mask << 1
      gbTimer_edgeIn = period - (next & (period - 1))
      return
    }
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < cycles; i = i + 1) {
    div = (div + 1) & 0xffff

    let high = 0
    if ((div & mask) !== 0) { high = 1 }
    if (was !== 0 && high === 0) {
      // gbTimer_incTima inlined, with the window counter kept in the local.
      let t = (GB_IO[0x05] + 1) & 0xff
      GB_IO[0x05] = t
      if (t === 0) { pending = 4 }
    }
    was = high

    // Drain the post-overflow delay one cycle at a time. Note the order: an
    // increment on this same cycle arms the window at 4 and it is immediately
    // decremented to 3, which is what gives the window its four-cycle length
    // measured from the wrap.
    if (pending > 0) {
      pending = pending - 1
      if (pending === 0) {
        GB_IO[0x05] = GB_IO[0x06]
        GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_RELOADED] = 1
        // gbMmu_requestIrq is `GB_IO[0x0F] |= (1 << bit)` and reads none of the
        // timer's state, so the locals above stay authoritative across it.
        gbMmu_requestIrq(GB_IRQ_TIMER)
      }
    }
  }

  GB_CTR[GB_C_DIV] = div
  GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT] = was
  GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMA_PENDING] = pending
  gbTimer_syncLive()
}
